(*
Issue: a procedure declared inside a procedure declared inside another,
arbitrarily deep, recurses through parseBlock/parseProcDecl with no
depth limit, so before this test existed it exhausted the real C++
stack instead of failing cleanly (BUG B, distinct funnel point from the
compound-statement case in this directory: this one bounds nested
declarations, not nested statements).  700 levels is well past the
500-deep ceiling (MaxBlockDepth, ParseDecl.cpp), while staying safely
below the real crash threshold on this machine (empirically between
2,000 and 3,000 levels -- much lower than the ~20,000 for parenthesized
expressions or nested compound statements, because each level of
procedure nesting costs far more stack than one '(' or one 'begin').
The actual crash threshold is exercised end-to-end, under the real
compiler's real stack, by
test-lit/Driver/ParserRobustness/deeply-nested-procedure-declarations-do-not-crash-the-compiler.pas.

Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time -- lit has no
loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody should
have to look at 700 nested procedure declarations to read this file, so
the lit directives come first.

RUN: not %plang_ir -dump-parse-tree %s 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

